The specialist considers you for a moment, apparently deciding that you are owed a straight answer. "Picric acid stains skin yellow—you may already have some discoloration on your hands if you look." You look. Your palms have a faint yellowish cast you assumed was dust. "With prolonged skin contact at higher concentrations, it can affect liver and kidney function. Headaches, nausea. Potentially worse with significant exposure over time." She holds your gaze. "The concentration you've encountered today is not in the catastrophic range. But it's not nothing, and it accumulates."

"So I go yellow and my liver complains," you say.

"That's a simplified version." She begins laying out wash equipment on the plastic sheet. "The yellow is already happening. The liver concern is why we're not sending you home with a flannel and good wishes."

You look at your hands properly. The staining is faint but real, worked into the creases of your knuckles, into the scraped skin beneath the dressing. A hundred year old room leaving its mark on you.

Owen has stood up again behind the tape. He is looking at your hands from ten metres away with an expression you recognise now as the one he wore beside the locked gate, doing arithmetic he does not want to finish. Mara's hand remains on his shoulder.

"It washes out?" you ask.

"Mostly," the specialist says, which is not entirely reassuring.
