The decontamination continues. The specialist works up to your elbows with the methodical patience of someone who has learned not to rush chemistry. The yellow in your creases lightens but does not disappear entirely, settling into your skin like a watermark. She moves to your hair next, which takes considerably longer given the state of your bun, and you endure it without complaint because the alternative arguments have temporarily run out of momentum.

Your father, Mara tells you eventually, is coming. She says it neutrally, the way you might report weather. He is coming from the office and will meet you at the hospital because the EOD team wants a doctor to look at your liver function and run a blood panel, routine precaution given the exposure duration. She does not relay anything else he said, and you do not ask.

The specialist replaces your coverall with a fresh one, seals your clothes in a labelled bag for the disposal team, and hands you a pair of paper slip-on shoes because your trainers go into a second bag. You look down at them. They are several sizes too large and make a shuffling sound when you walk.

