The ambulance delivers you to a side entrance where a nurse in blue scrubs takes one look at your white coverall and yellow hands and directs you to a small assessment room that smells of antiseptic and floor cleaner. A doctor appears, asks questions about the exposure duration, the room dimensions, your approximate route through it, and whether you touched your face, then disappears again. A second nurse takes blood from the inside of your elbow with brisk efficiency and labels the vials without comment. Then everyone leaves and the waiting begins.

The assessment room has two plastic chairs, a paper-covered examination bed, and a laminated poster about hand hygiene that you study exhaustively within the first four minutes. Owen sits in one of the plastic chairs with his phone, ostensibly reading, actually checking it every ninety seconds. Mara finds you after twenty minutes and occupies the second chair, having apparently negotiated her way past the side entrance with the particular determination of someone who has decided that rules are a conversation rather than a wall. She brings three cups of vending machine tea in thin plastic cups. Yours is too sweet and slightly wrong but warm, and you drink all of it.

The paper on the examination bed crinkles every time you shift position, which is often, because waiting is genuinely terrible and the paper shoes make everything worse.

Your father arrives forty-three minutes after you do. You hear him before you see him, his voice in the corridor carrying the clipped efficiency he uses when he is uncomfortable and converting it into motion. He appears in the doorway in his work coat, takes in the coverall and the yellow hands and Owen and Mara occupying both chairs, and stops.

